## Claim
Text: "A “new virus” disease called Novel Bunya V!rus that is caused by ticks is diagnosed as severe fever with thrombocytopenia syndrome (SFTS) is spreading in China."
Claim date: September 29, 2020
Claim origin: https://perma.cc/JH67-EM3U?type=image

## Initial Q&A
### Is there a virus scientifically recognized as the "Novel Bunya Virus" that was identified prior to or around September 29, 2020?
Answer: Yes, a virus scientifically recognized as a novel Bunyavirus (specifically designated SFTS bunyavirus or SFTSV) was identified and characterized in research published prior to 2020. According to the source, "Heightened surveillance of acute febrile illness in China since 2009 has led to the identification of... SFTS," and the landmark study was published in the *New England Journal of Medicine* in 2011, describing a "novel virus... designated SFTS bunyavirus (SFTSV)" that is a member of the *Bunyaviridae* family.

Source URL: https://www.ecdc.europa.eu/en/news-events/fever-thrombocytopenia-associated-novel-bunyavirus-china

### Is the medical condition known as Severe Fever with Thrombocytopenia Syndrome (SFTS) primarily transmitted to humans through tick bites?
Answer: Yes, the medical condition known as Severe Fever with Thrombocytopenia Syndrome (SFTS) is primarily transmitted to humans through tick bites, as ticks serve as a key vector for the causative virus.

Source URL: https://virologyj.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s12985-020-01391-1

### Has the pathogen that causes SFTS been scientifically described as a novel bunya-type virus?
Answer: Yes, the pathogen causing SFTS has been scientifically described as a novel bunya-type virus, as evidenced by the landmark 2011 publication in the *New England Journal of Medicine* titled "Fever with thrombocytopenia associated with a novel bunyavirus in China."

Source URL: https://www.mdpi.com/1999-4915/12/9/958

### As of September 29, 2020, was there documented evidence of SFTS cases occurring in China?
Answer: Yes, as of September 29, 2020, there was well-documented evidence of SFTS cases occurring in China. The search result, a 2015 research paper, explicitly states that SFTS "was first identified in 2010" and that "Up to end of 2013, a total of 14 provinces reported" cases, with an integrated clinical dataset of 1920 patients constructed for analysis.

Source URL: https://scholar.archive.org/work/yrdj3dpqivcmndz7t4q4vaklwa/access/wayback/https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/646613339A27423E170E7FBD499ACDB9/S0950268815002678a.pdf/div-class-title-epidemiological-and-clinical-characteristics-of-severe-fever-with-thrombocytopenia-syndrome-sfts-in-china-an-integrated-data-analysis-div.pdf

### As of September 29, 2020, were health authorities in China reporting an active outbreak or a significant increase in cases of SFTS?
Answer: Yes, as of September 29, 2020, health authorities in China were reporting an active outbreak of SFTS. The search result indicates that SFTS cases first appeared in April 2020, with "37 more cases...in Jiangsu and 23 in Anhui Provinces in China" reported thereafter, indicating ongoing transmission and an increase in cases during that period.

Source URL: https://lymediseaseassociation.org/ticks/sfts-virus-outbreak-in-china-from-ticks/

### Prior to September 29, 2020, had the SFTS virus been documented as a public health concern in China?
Answer: Yes, prior to September 29, 2020, the SFTS virus had been documented as a public health concern in China. The source indicates that while SFTSV was first discovered in 2009, retrospective analysis confirmed it had been causing disease in China since at least a 1996 outbreak, and it was listed as a WHO priority disease in 2017.

Source URL: https://journals.plos.org/plosntds/article?id=10.1371/journal.pntd.0006603

### Did any official Chinese government or public health agency (e.g., the Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention) issue warnings or reports about SFTS spreading around September 29, 2020?
Answer: Yes, based on the search result, official Chinese media reported on the spread of SFTS around that time, with the state-run *Global Times* citing information about cases and deaths in Jiangsu and Anhui provinces and noting that "official media here reported" on the disease, including warnings about possible human-to-human transmission.

Source URL: https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/seven-dead-60-infected-by-new-infectious-disease-in-china-says-media-reports/article32283026.ece

### Is the term "Novel Bunya Virus" used interchangeably with the SFTS virus in credible medical literature or public health advisories?
Answer: No, the provided credible medical literature does not use the term "Novel Bunya Virus" interchangeably with the SFTS virus. The source consistently and exclusively refers to the causative agent as "severe fever with thrombocytopenia bunyavirus (SFTSV)," which is classified within the Bunyavirales order.

Source URL: https://academic.oup.com/ofid/article/6/6/ofz209/5488745

### What was the known geographical distribution of SFTS cases within China as of late 2020?
Answer: As of late 2020, the known geographical distribution of SFTS cases in China showed the highest incidence concentrated in five provinces: Henan, Shandong, Hubei, and Anhui in northern and central China, along with Zhejiang in the southeast Yangtze River Delta region.

Source URL: https://journals.plos.org/plosntds/article?id=10.1371/journal.pntd.0008186

### What is the known mortality rate or severity of SFTS as described by medical authorities prior to September 29, 2020?
Answer: Prior to September 29, 2020, medical authorities described the mortality rate of SFTS as ranging from **11.2% to 30%**, with severe cases often resulting in death due to multiple organ failure.

Source URL: https://journals.plos.org/plosntds/article?id=10.1371/journal.pntd.0007829

## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the key findings are that a novel bunyavirus (officially designated SFTSV) was identified as the cause of Severe Fever with Thrombocytopenia Syndrome (SFTS) in China in 2010/2011, with the disease being transmitted primarily by ticks. As of September 29, 2020, SFTS was a documented and ongoing public health concern in China, with reports of an active outbreak and cases occurring across multiple provinces. The mortality rate was known to be significant. The term "Novel Bunya Virus" is not used in the standard scientific nomenclature for SFTSV, but the claim's description of the virus, its mode of transmission, and the associated disease is accurate.

The evidence is very clear on the core facts: the virus exists, causes SFTS, is tick-borne, and was spreading in China at the time of the claim. The only minor discrepancy is the specific colloquial name "Novel Bunya Virus," which is not the standard scientific term but is a plausible layperson's description of a novel virus within the *Bunyavirales* order. This does not constitute a substantial inaccuracy that refutes the claim.

Therefore, the claim's assertions are strongly supported by the documented evidence. `supported`

### Verdict: SUPPORTED

### Justification
The claim is **supported** by evidence, as Severe Fever with Thrombocytopenia Syndrome (SFTS) is caused by a novel bunyavirus (officially designated SFTSV), which was [scientifically identified in China in 2010/2011](https://www.ecdc.europa.eu/en/news-events/fever-thrombocytopenia-associated-novel-bunyavirus-china). This tick-borne virus [causes a high fever with low platelet count](https://virologyj.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s12985-020-01391-1) and has a significant mortality rate. By late September 2020, SFTS was an [active and spreading public health concern in China](https://lymediseaseassociation.org/ticks/sfts-virus-outbreak-in-china-from-ticks/), with cases reported across multiple provinces and [official warnings issued](https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/seven-dead-60-infected-by-new-infectious-disease-in-china-says-media-reports/article32283026.ece). While the colloquial name "Novel Bunya Virus" is not the standard scientific nomenclature, the underlying facts about the virus, its transmission, and its spread are accurate.